{"records":[{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"54v7hn8hvz","title":"Legionella pneumophila exploits the endo-lysosomal network for phagosome biogenesis","description":"L. pneumophila strains harboring wild-type rpsL such as Lp02rpsLWT cannot replicate in mouse bone marrow-derived macrophages (BMDMs) due to induction of extensive lysosome damage and apoptosis. The mechanism of this unique infection-induced cell death remains unknown. Using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screening, we identified Hmg20a and Nol9 as host factors important for restricting strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Depletion of Hmg20a protects macrophages from infection-induced lysosomal damage and apoptosis, allowing productive bacterial replication. The restriction imposed by Hmg20a was mediated by repressing the expression of several endo-lysosomal proteins, including the small GTPase Rab7. We found that SUMOylated Rab7 is recruited to the bacterial phagosome via SulF, a Dot/Icm effector that harbors a SUMO-interacting motif (SIM). Moreover, overexpression of Rab7 rescues intracellular growth of strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Our results establish that L. pneumophila exploits the lysosomal network for the biogenesis of its phagosome in BMDMs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-07T16:49:43.441Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54v7hn8hvz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54v7hn8hvz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chuang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bkwjctz23n","title":"Regulation of phosphoribosyl-linked serine ubiquitination by deubiquitinases DupA and DupB, Shin et al","description":"The family of bacterial SidE enzymes catalyzes non-canonical phosphoribosyl-linked (PR) serine ubiquitination and promotes infectivity of Legionella pneumophila, a pathogenic bacterium causing Legionnaires’ disease. Here we describe identification of two bacterial effectors (LaiE and LaiF) that actively reverse PR-ubiquitination during infection and are thus named DeUbiquitinases for PR-ubiquitination (DUPs; DupA/LaiE and DupB/LaiF). Structural analyses revealed that DupA and SidE ubiquitin ligases harbor a highly homologous catalytic phosphodiesterase (PDE) domain. However, unlike SidE ubiquitin ligases, DupA displays increased affinity to PR-ubiquitinated substrates, which dictates its catalytic activity allowing DupA to predominantly cleave PR-ubiquitin from substrates. Interfering with DupA-ubiquitin binding switches its activity toward SidE-type ligase. Given the high affinity of DupA to PR-ubiquitinated substrates, we next exploited a catalytically inactive DupA mutant to trap and subsequently identify more than 180 PR-ubiquitinated host proteins in Legionella-infected cells. Proteins involved in endoplasmic reticulum (ER) fragmentation and membrane recruitment to Legionella-containing vacuoles (LCV) emerged as major SidE targets. The global map of PR-ubiquitinated substrates uncovered by DupA-mediated trapping strategy provides critical insights into host-pathogen interactions during Legionella infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-31T15:44:44.544Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bkwjctz23n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bkwjctz23n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Dikic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Donghyuk","last_name":"Shin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rukmini","last_name":"Mukherjee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaobin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Gonzalez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Structural Biology","Endoplasmic Reticulum","Ubiquitylation","Legionella"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3jtdhjdbs","title":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Protein import motor complex reacts to mitochondrial misfolding by reducing protein import and activating mitophagy'","description":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Mitochondrial import loss induces mitophagy without membrane depolarization'. We hypothesized that inhibition of mitochondrial protein import induced mitophagy independent of mitochondrial depolarization. This dataset contains raw Illumina sequencing data for all samples from a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen for mitophagy regulators. The genome-wide library is Brunello from Doench et al Nat Biotechnol. 2016. We found that the pre-sequence translocase-associated motor complex PAM controls mitophagy induction. Loss of PAM caused a loss in protein import and was sufficient to induce mitophagy without depolarization.\nThe samples have been sequenced on an Illumina NextSeq 500 in single end mode with 75+8 cycles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-05T15:38:53.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3jtdhjdbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3jtdhjdbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melinda","last_name":"Brunstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Süleyman","last_name":"Bozkurt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ludovico","last_name":"Alves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Wegner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Kaulich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Pohl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Münch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy","Genetic Screening","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6wvt558dwh","title":"Mofidi Bacteria Inactivation Database","description":"Database created from an open, systematic literature review extracting bacteria log reduction values for chemical and physical water treatment processes. Data show CxT (for chlorine, chloramine, chlorine dioxide, ozone, peracetic acid, performic acid), UV light dosage (low pressure, medium pressure, pulsed), and filtration (microfiltration, ultrafiltration, reverse-osmosis) requirements for corresponding bacteria log reduction. The primary focus of this research was to extract datapoints for bacteria (positive and negative Gram stain; facultative anaerobic and aerobic; rod-, coccoid-, comma- and s-shaped; motile and non-motile; and pathogenic) along with bacteria groupings that include total coliforms, fecal coliforms, and HPC bacteria. Legionella spp. were separated in two as either Legionella pneumophila or non-pneumophila Legionella. In addition to the above bacteria, the database also includes a limited amount of treatment information for other organisms (secondary organisms) gathered circumstantially during the search: amoeba, fungi, bacterial spores, and radio-tolerant/extremophile bacteria. As these organisms were not the primary focus of this literature review, the recording of their treatment data should not be considered exhaustive. This database holds a total 8,031 records (4,822 bacteria and 3,209 secondary organisms).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-21T15:16:32.171Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6wvt558dwh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6wvt558dwh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"Mofidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Filtration","Water Treatment","Bacteria","Disinfection","Virus Inactivation","Legionella","Mycobacterial Infection","Amoeba","UV Disinfection"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":131409,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":149,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":131409,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130849,"restricted":64,"embargoed":496},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2156},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1387},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":612},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":453},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":408},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":364},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":290},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":264}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":128802},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":181},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":130},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":72},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c